  "verdict": {
    "verdict": "holds_with_qualification",
    "one_line": "The within-group association between PE share and total particle concentration is real at the full range and survives depth adjustment and permutation, but it is NOT a graded relationship across the concentration range: it is driven by the sub-1000/L transition, it is statistically fragile at G=5, and the varnish 'decline' is arithmetically the mirror of the PE rise rather than independent evidence.",
    "holds_because": [
      "Full-data FE slope +29.85 pp/decade reproduces exactly; depth-partialled permutation p ~ 1e-4; cyclic-shift permutation p=2e-4; leave-one-sample-out range 27.5-32.0 (all p<0.02); leave-one-group-out 26.0-34.8.",
      "The proposed 'PE-as-default-class at low N' artefact is refuted: low-N samples have LOW PE% (mean 23.6) and HIGH varnish% (mean 41.6), the opposite of the artefact's prediction.",
      "Above 1000/L the within-group Pearson r for total is 0.833 (R2 0.695), and the high-band slope stays positive (+17.1, p=0.13) albeit underpowered."
    ],
    "fails_or_weak_because": [
      "Abundance restriction destroys significance: total>1000 -> FE slope +11.5 (p=0.44), pooled rho +0.47 (p=0.076), mean within-group rho -0.08; total>3000 -> slope +2.0 (p=0.85), rho +0.35 (p=0.26). The effect is a low-abundance tail phenomenon, not a trend across the sampled range.",
      "High-band covariate specificity fails (b): above 1000/L, C:N (R2 0.691) and salinity (R2 0.526) match or approach total (0.695); this is not 'total is the driver' but a single dominant high-total sample per group in a 2-4 point within-group regression.",
      "At G=5 the nominal CR1 p=0.008 is not design-based: exact sign-flip p=0.15 (two-sided) / 0.09 (one-sided), wild cluster bootstrap p=0.13/0.065. The smallest attainable one-sided p at G=5 is 0.031, so the CR1 p-value is anti-conservative. Between-group heterogeneity is significant (Q p=0.0025, tau2=329) so the slopes do not estimate one common effect.",
      "'Varnish falls' is not independent evidence: within-group corr(PE,V)=-0.81 and each explains 65% of the other. This is one compositional axis with two names.",
      "PE is a bounded share saturating near 100% in the richest samples (median 99.7% above 10000/L); the constant pp-per-decade model is misspecified at the top of the range.",
      "The 'log10 PE count +1.17 (p=0.020)' alternative is arithmetically 1.0 + the PE-share slope; it is not independent evidence.",
      "group_05 is not a genuine regime difference: dropping a single sample (sample_025, PE=0) flips its slope from -1.16 to +12.97 and makes the group_05 interaction significant in the opposite direction; its PE tracks depth (rho +0.89, FE p=0.013) rather than total."
    ],
    "concrete_problems": [
      "Leverage: 66% of the FE numerator comes from samples at or below 1000/L that make up half the data; the single most influential points are the low-abundance anchors of each group (sample_001, sample_006, sample_012, sample_021).",
      "The claim's cluster inference is anti-conservative at G=5.",
      "Group_05's exception is a one-point artefact of sample_025.",
      "The varnish half of the claim is tautological given the PE half.",
      "'log10 PE count' is redundant with the share outcome.",
      "PE% saturates, so the pooled rho is partly generated by the 3 samples above 10000/L where PE ~ 99-100%."
    ],
    "what_would_settle_it": "Independent samples with total >1000/L spanning >=1 decade within station, or a station-level design with more than 5 clusters; both are needed to separate the low-abundance transition from a graded concentration effect."
  }
